.TH tupdf n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tupdf
.PP
Read\-only PDF structure inspector in pure Tcl.
.PP
\fBtupdf\fR scans PDF files for common structural information: header version, object ids, page markers, trailer text, metadata, and raw objects. Since \fB0.2\fR it also inflates compressed object streams (\fB/ObjStm\fR) with the built\-in \fBzlib\fR and reads the trailer from a cross\-reference stream (\fB/Type /XRef\fR), so objects, pages and metadata hidden inside them are resolved too. It is still a lightweight inspector, not a full PDF parser.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tclutils::tupdf 0.2
.CE
.SS API
.CS
::tclutils::tupdf::version pdfFile
::tclutils::tupdf::summary pdfFile
::tclutils::tupdf::metadata pdfFile
::tclutils::tupdf::trailer pdfFile
::tclutils::tupdf::objects pdfFile
::tclutils::tupdf::object pdfFile id
::tclutils::tupdf::zugferd pdfFile
.CE
.SS Example
.CS
package require tclutils::tupdf

puts [::tclutils::tupdf::version doc.pdf]
puts [::tclutils::tupdf::summary doc.pdf]
puts [::tclutils::tupdf::zugferd doc.pdf]
puts [::tclutils::tupdf::object doc.pdf 3]
.CE
.SS "ZUGFeRD / Factur\-X"
.PP
\fBzugferd\fR applies lightweight heuristics on the raw file bytes:
.IP \(bu
embedded files (\fB/EmbeddedFile\fR, \fB/Filespec\fR, \fB/AF\fR)
.IP \(bu
invoice XML attachment names (\fBZUGFeRD\-invoice.xml\fR, \fBfactur\-x.xml\fR, …)
.IP \(bu
MIME markers (\fBapplication/vnd.zugferd…\fR, \fBapplication/pdf+xml\fR)
.IP \(bu
invoice XML snippets (\fBCrossIndustryInvoice\fR, …)
.PP
Returns a dict: \fBdetected\fR, \fBprofile\fR (\fBzugferd\fR, \fBfactur\-x\fR, \fBunknown\fR, or \fB""\fR), \fBattachmentNames\fR, \fBmimeTypes\fR, \fBhints\fR.
.PP
This is \fBnot\fR a PDF/A\-3 or EN 16931 conformance check. Since 0.2 attachments referenced from compressed object streams are resolved; attachment \fIcontents\fR inside encrypted streams remain invisible.
.PP
\fBsummary\fR adds \fBzugferdDetected\fR (and \fBzugferdProfile\fR when detected).
.SS "Compressed PDFs (0.2)"
.PP
Modern PDFs pack most objects into zlib\-compressed \fBobject streams\fR (\fB/Type /ObjStm\fR) and replace the classic trailer with a \fBcross\-reference\fR\fB \fR\fBstream\fR (\fB/Type /XRef\fR). \fBtupdf\fR 0.2 handles both:
.IP \(bu
\fB/ObjStm\fR streams are inflated (\fBzlib\fR) and their embedded objects are exposed as normal \fBN 0 obj … endobj\fR text, so \fBobjects\fR, \fBobject\fR, \fBmetadata\fR and the page count in \fBsummary\fR see them.
.IP \(bu
\fBtrailer\fR falls back to the \fB/Type /XRef\fR dictionary (\fB/Root\fR, \fB/Info\fR, \fB/Size\fR) when there is no \fBtrailer\fR keyword.
.PP
As a result, \fBpages\fR and \fBobjects\fR are exact on Flate\-compressed PDFs, not just lower bounds. No external tool (qpdf) is required.
.SS Limitations
.PP
The binary cross\-reference table is not decoded (its plaintext dictionary is enough for the trailer). Object streams with a PNG/TIFF \fBpredictor\fR are rare and skipped \-\- counts then fall back to a lower bound. \fBEncrypted\fR streams are not decrypted, and attachments inside encrypted streams stay invisible. For deep inspection, convert to QDF with qpdf or use a full PDF parser.
